plan ProjectDelivery
title   Capability delivery order
status  proposed pending approval
goal
    Give the twelve proposed capability Plans one entry point for ownership, integration edges,
    and staged execution. Preserve their semantic ownership, current Task status, and the separate
    approval gates for implementation and package cutover.
outcomes
    O1  Capability ownership and integration map
        Each capability Plan owns its requirements, API, behavior, exclusions, and proof targets.
        This entry Plan records their handoffs without restating their contracts.

        - @plan.Statecharts.O1 through @plan.Statecharts.O4 own definitions, machine grammar,
        ordered turns, timers, and declarations. Applications and operations consume them.
        - @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O1 through @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O7 own the App graph,
        Runtime, actors, leases, context, and current cuts. This is the production execution and
        publication boundary for hosts, operations, and evidence.
        - @plan.DiagnosticsContract.O1 through @plan.DiagnosticsContract.O6 own diagnostic data,
        codes, mapping, classification, host projections, and rendering. All other owners use
        this shared failure language.
        - @plan.Resources.O1 through @plan.Resources.O4 own resource descriptors, canonical keys
        and data, acquisition, generations, and writes. They share store and preview behavior
        with @plan.Transactions.O3.
        - @plan.Transactions.O1 through @plan.Transactions.O4 own finite mutations, concurrency,
        previews, cancellation, and remote uncertainty. Durable reconciliation crosses
        @plan.Persistence.O4.
        - @plan.Streams.O1 through @plan.Streams.O3 own continuing values, session ownership,
        projections, release, and durable projections. They use Statechart declarations and
        Runtime lifetimes; explicit writes use @plan.Resources.O4.
        - @plan.InspectionAndArtifacts.O1 through @plan.InspectionAndArtifacts.O6 own inspection,
        shared codecs, bounded evidence, trace schemas, exports, and comparison. They consume
        current cuts from @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O7; their codec supports
        @plan.Persistence.O1.
        - @plan.Persistence.O1 through @plan.Persistence.O5 own providers, durable selection,
        capture, hydration, and ordered writes. Hosts and Stories consume these boundaries.
        - @plan.ReactAndHosts.O1 through @plan.ReactAndHosts.O4 own Runtime attachment, selectors,
        SSR, and host bridges. They adapt the existing Runtime.
        - @plan.StoriesAndModels.O1 through @plan.StoriesAndModels.O6 own Story builders, fixtures,
        execution, checkpoints, pure models, and the gateway. The CLI consumes that gateway.
        - @plan.CommandLine.O1 through @plan.CommandLine.O4 own process grammar, gateway loading,
        artifact I/O, signals, and exits. They use @plan.StoriesAndModels.O6 and
        @plan.InspectionAndArtifacts.O2, @plan.InspectionAndArtifacts.O4, and
        @plan.InspectionAndArtifacts.O6.
        - @plan.PackageContract.O1 through @plan.PackageContract.O4 own package routes, packed
        consumers, compatibility disposition, and promotion gates. Final promotion waits for
        @plan.PackageContract.V3 and @plan.PackageContract.V4.

        The ten former flat Plans and Rewrite Task graph are archived as historical provenance.
        Their IDs and records remain in the archive; archived Plan IDs remain discoverable for
        existing Spec references. This capability set and this entry remain proposed and establish
        no active execution authority.
    O2  Contract dependencies are distinct from executable prerequisites
        Align shared type and evidence interfaces before implementation. An ownership citation
        identifies the semantic owner; it does not create an enforced Plan dependency or say the
        cited outcome has passed.

        Coordinate these contract edges as paired design and compile slices:

        - @plan.Statecharts.O1 supplies machine declarations and provider selectors;
        @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O1 and @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O6 define their closed
        application graph and fixed context bindings.
        - @plan.Statecharts.O3 defines accepted event actions; @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O7
        defines the complete publication cut. Agree the interface together before either owner
        claims a complete turn implementation.
        - @plan.Resources.O1, @plan.Transactions.O1, and @plan.Streams.O1 define operation types
        against the machine event and App.M types before their executable kernels are integrated.
        - @plan.DiagnosticsContract.O1 and @plan.DiagnosticsContract.O5 define shared diagnostic
        data and vocabularies early. @plan.InspectionAndArtifacts.O2 supplies the shared codec;
        define evidence records before Runtime turns depend on evidence acceptance.
        - @plan.PackageContract.O1 establishes route ownership early. Route, declaration, and
        packed-consumer checks continue with every public change.
        - @plan.StoriesAndModels.O6 defines the inert gateway consumed by
        @plan.CommandLine.O1. Agree their selection and execution boundary together; implement
        the gateway before CLI execution.
        - @plan.Resources.O4 and @plan.ReactAndHosts.O4 share the construction-owned setData
        authority. Agree that contract seam before operation execution in Stage 3; implement host
        adapters in Stage 5.

        Implement runtime behavior through one production Runtime. Stage actor turns before
        operation kernels, then integrate their store changes and evidence into the same publication
        owner. Do not create parallel actor, store, evidence, Story, or CLI semantics to avoid a
        cross-owner dependency.
    O3  Staged execution follows the prerequisite DAG
        This proposed order preserves the dependency shape of archived @plan.Verification.D1.
        Until this entry is approved and its order is adopted through Task reconciliation, these
        stages remain proposed and do not change current Task readiness. Use them to prepare the
        Task decomposition. A stage may contain parallel owner work after its stated prerequisites.
        Each gate is an exit condition for its Task increment, not a claim that a whole Plan 
        outcome,
        Verification proof source, or stage has passed. Capability Plans own acceptance through
        their Verification entries; each exit gate applies only to the clauses assigned to that
        stage, with later clauses deferred to their owning stages.

        1. **Static foundation.** Build @plan.DiagnosticsContract.O1 and
        @plan.DiagnosticsContract.O5, @plan.Statecharts.O1 and @plan.Statecharts.O2,
        @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O1 and @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O2, operation type and
        key slices, shared codec and evidence interfaces, and initial
        @plan.PackageContract.O1 routes.
        - **Gate:** Pinned compiler fixtures preserve accepted literals and reject invalid
        shapes. Foundational diagnostic, identity, key, and structural-codec proofs pass.
        Route and declaration checks pass. Owner targets are
        @plan.DiagnosticsContract.V1, @plan.Statecharts.V1, @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.V1,
        @plan.Resources.V1, @plan.Transactions.V1, @plan.Streams.V1,
        @plan.InspectionAndArtifacts.V2, and @plan.PackageContract.V1.
        2. **Runtime ownership.** Build no-persistence base readiness, closed admission, actor
        leases, mailbox turns, context, lifecycle, and actor-only publication on the static
        foundation. Integrate @plan.Statecharts.O3 with @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O7 and
        initial evidence acceptance. Persistence-backed bootstrap and restore follow in Stage 4.
        - **Gate:** Production-owner checks prove base readiness, turn ordering, actor snapshots,
        failure lanes, and cleanup. This stage covers
        @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.V3 through @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.V7,
        @plan.Statecharts.V3 and @plan.Statecharts.V4, and @plan.DiagnosticsContract.V2.
        Persistence membership, restore, ordered-write, and final-flush clauses are Stage 4 work.
        The actor-only cut is intermediate; Stages 3–4 integrate operation and persistence evidence.
        3. **Operation kernels.** Agree the construction-owned setData authority with
        @plan.ReactAndHosts.O4 as an interface seam; add the Runtime-owned canonical resource
        store, generations, transaction policies, previews, and stream sessions through accepted
        event actions. Host adapters remain Stage 5 work. Integrate resource writes and operation
        evidence into the same actor publication cut.
        - **Gate:** Owner tests prove key identity, passive reads, fencing, cancellation,
        preview isolation, explicit writes, stream release, and one complete actor/store/
        evidence cut. This completes the actor/store publication clauses of
        @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.V7. Owner targets are @plan.Resources.V2 through
        @plan.Resources.V4, @plan.Transactions.V2 and @plan.Transactions.V3,
        @plan.Streams.V2, and @plan.InspectionAndArtifacts.V3. Hydration and no-replay clauses
        remain Stage 4 work.
        4. **Persistence and complete evidence.** Add persistence-backed bootstrap and restore,
        durable selection, consistent capture, no-replay hydration, ordered writes, bounded
        evidence, and artifact schemas and codecs. This needs the Runtime and operation cuts plus
        the shared codec.
        - **Gate:** Capture observes one committed cut; restore does not replay work; failed
        writes retain the last good record; capture never sees a half actor/store commit; and
        artifact and trace bounds and round trips pass. Owner targets are
        @plan.Persistence.V2 through @plan.Persistence.V5,
        @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.V3, @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.V6 and
        @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.V7, @plan.InspectionAndArtifacts.V2 through
        @plan.InspectionAndArtifacts.V5, and @plan.StoriesAndModels.V4. React and CLI integration
        remain Stage 5 work.
        5. **Hosts, Stories, and CLI.** Integrate React, Stories/models, and the installed CLI over
        the production Runtime and evidence. React attachment and selectors may start after
        Stage 2; SSR waits for persistence. Story execution waits for Stages 2 through 4. CLI
        waits for the Story gateway and artifact comparison.
        - **Gate:** Browser and isolated-consumer checks, live Story/model parity, installed
        binary behavior, output, signal, and exit proofs pass. Run proving applications under
        @plan.PackageContract.O4. Owner targets are @plan.ReactAndHosts.V1 through
        @plan.ReactAndHosts.V4, @plan.StoriesAndModels.V1 through @plan.StoriesAndModels.V6,
        @plan.CommandLine.V1 through @plan.CommandLine.V4, @plan.InspectionAndArtifacts.V1 and
        @plan.InspectionAndArtifacts.V6, and @plan.PackageContract.V2 and @plan.PackageContract.V4.
        6. **Package cutover and absence.** Complete package promotion, compatibility disposition,
        physical cleanup, and product cutover only after all owner proofs and consumer gates.
        This requires every prior stage.
        - **Gate:** Packed consumers, browser and package gates pass; runtime exports and
        declarations prove deleted surfaces absent; rejected inputs fail before side effects;
        the no-residue audit and final receipts pass. Owner targets are
        @plan.PackageContract.V1, @plan.PackageContract.V3 and @plan.PackageContract.V4.

        Critical path: foundation, Runtime, operations, persistence/evidence, hosts, then cutover.
        React attachment/selectors can start after Runtime ownership. Operation owners may work in
        parallel after action, key, store, and publication seams are agreed; integrated proofs use
        one production owner. Run package type and packed-consumer checks at every public-surface
        change.

        The dependency cycles are resolved inside coherent increments. @plan.Statecharts.O3 and
        @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O7 share one turn/publication increment. @plan.Resources.O2 and
        @plan.Transactions.O3 share one canonical-store/preview increment. The evidence interface is
        agreed in Stage 1, then @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O7,
        @plan.InspectionAndArtifacts.O3, and @plan.Transactions.O2 integrate their producers and
        acceptance in Stages 2 and 3. @plan.Streams.O2 and @plan.Resources.O4 integrate explicit
        stream writes through the same setData path in Stage 3. These cycles do not create alternate
        owners or require unrelated capability work to wait. Agree the construction-owned setData
        seam with @plan.ReactAndHosts.O4 in Stage 3; its host adapters remain in Stage 5. Stage 2
        provides base readiness without persistence; Stage 4 adds the @plan.Persistence.O2 boot and
        restore sequence. Pair the
        @plan.StoriesAndModels.O6 and @plan.CommandLine.O1 gateway contract, then implement the
        gateway before its CLI consumer in Stage 5.
    O4  Tasks and local gates realize the order
        Workflow Tasks remain the executable status and readiness graph. Decompose capability
        outcomes into Tasks with concrete acceptance and focused checks. Link each Task to its
        semantic owner outcome and add Task-to-Task blockers for the prerequisites in @O3. Do not
        treat Plan citations or this staged order as enforced Task links. Do not change existing
        Task status or claim a Task complete from this entry Plan.

        Keep unresolved choices at their owning Plan and gate only affected Tasks:

        - @plan.Resources.Q1 leaves resource authoring forms and omitted freshness policy open;
        unrelated key, store, and settled reader work can continue.
        - @plan.Transactions.Q2 leaves the omitted concurrency policy open; tasks needing that
        default wait for its owner decision.
        - @plan.Streams.Q1 leaves adapter return and mapper-container forms open; independent
        lifetime and release work can continue.
        - @plan.InspectionAndArtifacts.Q2 leaves selected final call forms open; shared codec and
        already specified evidence behavior can proceed.
        The archived @plan.Runtime.Q1, @plan.Operations.Q1, @plan.Hosts.Q1, and
        @plan.Diagnostics.Q1 remain unresolved in their records. This entry does not carry or
        resolve them as current capability decisions.

        The archived @plan.Verification.Q1 and @plan.Cutover.Q1 retain unresolved root-gate and
        product-cutover questions as historical references. This entry does not answer or adopt
        them as current gates. All capability Plans and this entry remain proposed pending approval.
decisions
    D1  One entry Plan owns cross-capability ordering
        This Plan proposes the relative order and integration map for the twelve capability Plans.
        It preserves the dependency shape of archived @plan.Verification.D1 as historical
        provenance. The capability Plans remain the semantic and proof owners through their
        Verification entries. Workflow Tasks own executable status, concrete blockers, and receipts.
    D2  Public package validation stays continuous
        Validate route and declaration reachability whenever a public surface changes. Use focused
        rewrite type and packed-consumer checks throughout delivery; reserve full browser, installed
        package, and workspace closeout for their owning integration and cutover gates.
constraints
    C1  Proposed order grants no implementation or cutover approval
        This Plan is an entry and ordering proposal. It does not approve capability Plans, authorize
        implementation, change Task state, promote the package, or complete product cutover.
        Package promotion and product cutover require the approved owner gates and their executable
        receipts.
    C2  Preserve semantic owners and existing records
        Do not duplicate capability API semantics here. Keep the former package frozen and
        historical source records unchanged. The ten former flat Plans and Rewrite Task graph are
        archived as historical provenance; existing Specs remain unchanged and their archived Plan
        references still resolve.
verification
    V1  Owner links and cited prerequisites resolve
        Establish @O1 and @O2 by checking that all twelve capability Plans are listed, every cited
        Plan object exists, and each integration edge points to its owning Plan. Check:
        `workflow check` and a review of the cited Plan IDs. This verifies artifact and reference
        integrity only; it does not establish implementation behavior.
    V2  Stage graph is topological and gates are observable
        Establish @O3 and @O4 by checking the explicit stage prerequisites in order and reviewing
        that each cycle is integrated within one owner increment rather than represented as a
        circular stage dependency. Confirm every exit gate names observable behavior and its
        capability Verification owners. Check: `workflow check` plus manual DAG review against
        the stage dependencies and cited owner Verification entries. This is a planning check, not
        evidence that a stage has run.
    V3  Package and cutover status remain pending
        Establish @O4 and @C1 by confirming no package promotion or product cutover occurs before
        the approved owner gates have executable receipts. Check: `workflow check` and review of
        the owner records. A green Workflow check does not prove consumer behavior, no-residue, or
        cutover.
